Section 12(1) grounds:
(a) non-consummation owing to the respondent’s impotence;
(b) contravention of s. 5(ii) mental capacity;
(c) consent obtained by force or by fraud as to the nature of the ceremony or a material fact concerning the respondent;
(d) respondent pregnant by another at the time of marriage.

A voidable marriage is valid until annulled. The decree is constitutive.

Section 12(2) bars: for force/fraud — petition more than one year after force ceased or fraud discovered, or full-consent cohabitation after that point, bars the petition. For pregnancy — ignorance at marriage, petition within one year of marriage, and no consensual intercourse after discovery.

These bars are mandatory conditions of maintainability.

No case authority invented. Case-law verification remains a separate gate.
